refs += [dict(id='planar',title='Planar F-Deletion: Approximation, Kernelization and Optimal FPT Algorithms',authors='Fedor V. Fomin; Daniel Lokshtanov; Neeldhara Misra; Saket Saurabh',year=2012,url='https://doi.org/10.1109/FOCS.2012.62',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.4230',locator='FOCS 2012, pages 470–479. Full author version arXiv:1204.4230v2, 2 November 2020, Theorem 2: polynomial kernel when the fixed family contains a planar graph. The randomized approximation step is distinguished by the later 2026 source.'),dict(id='lossy',title='Lossy Planarization: A Constant-Factor Approximate Kernelization for Planar Vertex Deletion',authors='Bart M. P. Jansen; Michał Włodarczyk',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.1137/22M152058X',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.02174',locator='SIAM Journal on Computing 54(1):1–91 (2025), following STOC 2022. Read the full arXiv:2202.02174v1 of 4 February 2022 and the 2026 paper’s description of the journal result: constant-factor approximate kernel, not an exact decision kernel.'),dict(id='structural',title='Kernelization Dichotomies for Hitting Minors Under Structural Parameterizations',authors='Marin Bougeret; Eric Brandwein; Ignasi Sau',year=2026,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.STACS.2026.17',pdf_url='https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol364-stacs2026/LIPIcs.STACS.2026.17/LIPIcs.STACS.2026.17.pdf',locator='Introduction, pages 17:2–17:3: general solution-size question and planarization remain open; original planar-family kernel plus Gupta et al.’s deterministic approximation yields a deterministic kernel. Theorem 1 lifts an already-existing kernel to structural parameters for connected forbidden graphs.')]
save(i,dict(title='Polynomial kernels for forbidden-minor vertex deletion',formal=r'Does vertex deletion to exclude any fixed finite family of graph minors admit a polynomial kernel, parameterized by the number of vertices allowed to be deleted? Precisely, for every fixed finite family \(\mathcal F\), is there a deterministic polynomial-time transformation of \((G,k)\) into an equivalent instance \((G^{\prime},k^{\prime})\) of the same \(\mathcal F\)-deletion problem whose total encoding length and parameter are bounded by \((k+1)^{O_{\mathcal F}(1)}\)?',definitions=r'''The family \(\mathcal F\) is fixed before inputs are supplied; it is not part of the parameterized input. Its members are finite nonempty undirected graphs and may be disconnected. The original source allows parallel edges, as in its two-vertex examples. Use finite undirected loopless multigraphs with all edge copies explicitly listed for the general convention here; simple graphs are included.

A graph \(H\) is a minor of \(G\) if it can be obtained from a subgraph by contracting edges. Contraction identifies their endpoints, removes resulting loops and retains parallel edges. It is not induced-subgraph containment or topological-minor containment.

The input consists of \(G\) and a nonnegative integer \(k\). It is a yes-instance if there is \(S\subseteq V(G)\) with \(|S|\leq k\) such that \(G-S\) contains no member of \(\mathcal F\) as a minor. All vertices have unit deletion cost. There is no planarity, degree, connectivity or sparsity promise on \(G\). The empty forbidden family is trivial and may be omitted. Since every forbidden graph is nonempty, \(k\geq |V(G)|\) is immediately a yes-instance.

A polynomial kernel is a deterministic algorithm that runs in a polynomial in the total binary input length and outputs a single pair \((G^{\prime},k^{\prime})\) for the same fixed family. It must preserve the yes/no answer exactly on every input. There must be constants \(C_{\mathcal F},c_{\mathcal F}\) such that the total bit length of the output and the numerical value \(k'\) are at most \(C_{\mathcal F}(k+1)^{c_{\mathcal F}}\). Vertex labels can be renumbered. Counting only vertices while leaving arbitrarily many explicit parallel edges or a large encoded parameter would not suffice.

The algorithm, its polynomial running-time degree and the output exponent may depend on \(\mathcal F\). The question does not require one exponent that works for all families, nor an effective procedure constructing the kernel algorithm from an arbitrary family description. Such uniformity requirements would strengthen the target.

The parameter is the solution budget \(k\). It is not the size of a supplied vertex cover, a set whose removal leaves bounded treedepth, or another structural parameter. A kernel supplies one smaller equivalent instance, unlike an algorithm querying many small instances. Approximate kernels that lose a constant factor in deletion cost do not satisfy the exact equivalence requirement.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove a polynomial kernel for every fixed finite forbidden family in this solution-budget model, allowing family-dependent exponents, or establish that some fixed family does not admit one. A conventional lower bound of the form “a polynomial kernel would imply NP is contained in coNP/poly” must retain that complexity assumption and is a conditional obstruction, not an unconditional separation. Special-family algorithms and approximate or structural-parameter kernels alone do not establish the universal claim.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Fixed-parameter tractability does not fix the kernel size',text='The source observes that every fixed-family problem is fixed-parameter tractable. This guarantees preprocessing to a size depending only on the solution budget, but does not guarantee polynomial dependence.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Families with a planar obstruction are covered',text='The FOCS 2012 work obtains polynomial kernels when the forbidden family contains a planar graph. The 2026 introduction explains how a later deterministic approximation removes the randomized ingredient from that kernel construction.',citation='structural'),dict(title='Planarization is a remaining special case',text=r'For \(\mathcal F=\{K_5,K_{3,3}\}\), the output after deletion must be planar. Both obstructions are nonplanar, so the preceding theorem does not apply. The later constant-factor approximate kernel does not preserve the exact decision threshold.',citation='lossy'),dict(title='The 2026 structural theorem keeps an existence premise',text='Theorem 1 transfers an existing solution-size kernel to a different parameterization for connected forbidden families. It does not supply the missing kernel for every family, and its introduction explicitly retains the general and planarization questions.',citation='structural')],why='Would give a general guarantee that graphs close to a minor-closed class can be reduced efficiently to a polynomial amount of relevant information. It unifies preprocessing questions for vertex cover, feedback vertex set, bounded-width deletion and planarization.',progress=[dict(date='2011',text='The source gives kernels for selected forbidden families and asks whether every fixed-family vertex-deletion problem has a polynomial kernel.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2012',text='The planar-obstruction condition yields a broad polynomial-kernel theorem.',citation='planar'),dict(date='2025',text='The journal version of lossy planarization gives a constant-factor approximate kernel, preserving the distinction from exact preprocessing.',citation='lossy'),dict(date='2026',text='STACS work extends structural parameterizations while explicitly retaining the general solution-budget question.',citation='structural')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the original minor and kernel definitions and full concluding question; checked the full planar-family theorem, the full 2022 lossy-planarization version and its 2025 journal status, and the 2026 structural theorem and open-status passage. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution for arbitrary fixed families. The deterministic requirement, family-dependent exponent and exact solution-budget parameter are explicit.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered polynomial kernelization as the missing predicate. Defined exact single-instance preprocessing, total encoding size, parallel-edge convention, parameter and family quantifiers. Distinguished planar obstruction from planar output and exact kernels from lossy or differently parameterized ones.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A major generic preprocessing question for graph-minor deletion, with vertex planarization among its unresolved instances. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Delete at most a specified number of vertices to eliminate every minor from a fixed finite family.','The question asks for polynomial-time preprocessing to one equivalent instance of polynomial size in that budget.','The polynomial may depend on the forbidden family.','Families containing a planar obstruction are covered, while planarization remains a key open case.','Later lossy and structural-parameter results do not establish the full exact-kernel claim.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','planar','lossy','structural'])),notes=['Restored polynomial kernelization as the main target and specified exact equivalence.','Defined fixed-family quantifiers, family-dependent exponents and total encoded kernel size.','Preserved the original parallel-edge minor convention.','Checked the planar-family result, its deterministic refinement and the precise limits of 2025–2026 progress.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.4230','https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.02174'])
